      "headline": "General Gasita Returns to Eastern DRC Command After Deadly Uvira Protests",
      "dek": "Brigade General Olivier Gasita, previously removed from Uvira following violent demonstrations in 2025, has been reassigned to intelligence duties in Lubumbashi.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Brigade General Olivier Gasita has returned to the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, taking up intelligence responsibilities in Lubumbashi. His new role within the 2nd Defense Zone of the FARDC comes less than a year after deadly protests in Uvira forced his recall to Kinshasa. General Gasita arrived in Lubumbashi on Saturday, August 22, alongside Major General Ngoie Kilubi Bob, to assume his duties overseeing military intelligence for a region encompassing multiple provinces.",
        "Gasita's previous assignment in Uvira began in September 2025 as head of operations and military intelligence for the 33rd Military Region. His presence there quickly drew significant opposition from segments of the population and civil society. On September 8, 2025, civil society members, supported by local armed groups known as Wazalendo, organized a protest demanding his departure. The situation escalated when demonstrators attempted to enter the FARDC headquarters, resulting in casualties.",
        "Civilian sources reported at least five deaths and twenty injuries during the Uvira protests. The FARDC confirmed the death of a 12-year-old child from a stray bullet and nine injuries, including four soldiers and five civilians. Gasita, of Banyamulenge origin, faced accusations of ties to Rwanda and the M23 rebel movement, though these claims were unsubstantiated. The protests occurred amid heightened ethnic tensions and anti-Tutsi rhetoric targeting the Banyamulenge community."
      ],
      "body": "Brigade General Olivier Gasita has returned to the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, taking up intelligence responsibilities in Lubumbashi. His new role within the 2nd Defense Zone of the FARDC comes less than a year after deadly protests in Uvira forced his recall to Kinshasa. General Gasita arrived in Lubumbashi on Saturday, August 22, alongside Major General Ngoie Kilubi Bob, to assume his duties overseeing military intelligence for a region encompassing multiple provinces.\n\nGasita's previous assignment in Uvira began in September 2025 as head of operations and military intelligence for the 33rd Military Region. His presence there quickly drew significant opposition from segments of the population and civil society. On September 8, 2025, civil society members, supported by local armed groups known as Wazalendo, organized a protest demanding his departure. The situation escalated when demonstrators attempted to enter the FARDC headquarters, resulting in casualties.\n\nCivilian sources reported at least five deaths and twenty injuries during the Uvira protests. The FARDC confirmed the death of a 12-year-old child from a stray bullet and nine injuries, including four soldiers and five civilians. Gasita, of Banyamulenge origin, faced accusations of ties to Rwanda and the M23 rebel movement, though these claims were unsubstantiated. The protests occurred amid heightened ethnic tensions and anti-Tutsi rhetoric targeting the Banyamulenge community.",
