      "headline": "Cameroon State Acquires General Bank, Raising Fiscal Concerns",
      "dek": "The African Development Bank warns of growing fiscal and governance risks as the state increases its stake in the banking sector, citing the recent acquisition of Société.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Cameroon's government has finalized its acquisition of Société Générale Cameroun, now renamed General Bank of Cameroon (GBC), increasing state control over the financial sector. The African Development Bank (AfDB) flagged this move in its 2026 Country Report, expressing concern over the expanding role of the state in banking. The AfDB noted that repeated public recapitalizations and acquisitions create additional fiscal and governance risks for the country.",
        "The report specifically cites the May 12, 2026, acquisition of the 58.08% stake previously held by Société Générale Group as the latest instance of this trend. With the state already holding a 25.60% share, this transaction significantly deepens government ownership. The AfDB's warning, however, does not suggest that GBC itself is financially weak but rather focuses on the broader implications of accumulating government stakes in banks.",
        "The central concern highlighted by the AfDB is the accumulation of government holdings in financial institutions, the associated governance risks, and the increasing interdependence between public finances and bank balance sheets. This trend raises questions about the long-term stability and independence of the banking sector within Cameroon as state influence grows."
      ],
      "body": "Cameroon's government has finalized its acquisition of Société Générale Cameroun, now renamed General Bank of Cameroon (GBC), increasing state control over the financial sector. The African Development Bank (AfDB) flagged this move in its 2026 Country Report, expressing concern over the expanding role of the state in banking. The AfDB noted that repeated public recapitalizations and acquisitions create additional fiscal and governance risks for the country.\n\nThe report specifically cites the May 12, 2026, acquisition of the 58.08% stake previously held by Société Générale Group as the latest instance of this trend. With the state already holding a 25.60% share, this transaction significantly deepens government ownership. The AfDB's warning, however, does not suggest that GBC itself is financially weak but rather focuses on the broader implications of accumulating government stakes in banks.\n\nThe central concern highlighted by the AfDB is the accumulation of government holdings in financial institutions, the associated governance risks, and the increasing interdependence between public finances and bank balance sheets. This trend raises questions about the long-term stability and independence of the banking sector within Cameroon as state influence grows.",
