      "articleId": "fec9bd8250d8b2270db7",
      "headline": "Equatorial Guinea Firms Lack Market Visibility Despite Value",
      "dek": "Many established companies in Equatorial Guinea possess significant value and operational history but struggle to translate this into public perception and market recognition.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Numerous companies in Equatorial Guinea, despite years of operation and substantial contributions to economic activities, remain largely unknown outside their immediate circles. This invisibility stems not from a lack of quality or value, but from an inability to project their achievements and offerings beyond their client and supplier base. The market perceives only a fraction of the internal decision-making, problem-solving, and value creation that occurs within these organizations.",
        "The challenge lies in the gap between possessing value and being perceived as valuable. While experience builds authority and results build reputation, these benefits are lost if not communicated effectively. Simply having a long market presence is not a substitute for a communication strategy. Companies often mistakenly believe that sporadic social media posts or occasional announcements constitute meaningful communication, rather than a consistent effort to answer who they are, what they do well, and why they matter.",
        "The core issue is not necessarily a need for companies to do more, but to make visible what they already do. Effective business communication should aim to build a consistent perception, answering fundamental questions about identity, core competencies, relevance, and memorability. Without this, the potential value generated internally remains unrecognized externally, hindering market position and growth opportunities."
      ],
      "body": "Numerous companies in Equatorial Guinea, despite years of operation and substantial contributions to economic activities, remain largely unknown outside their immediate circles. This invisibility stems not from a lack of quality or value, but from an inability to project their achievements and offerings beyond their client and supplier base. The market perceives only a fraction of the internal decision-making, problem-solving, and value creation that occurs within these organizations.\n\nThe challenge lies in the gap between possessing value and being perceived as valuable. While experience builds authority and results build reputation, these benefits are lost if not communicated effectively. Simply having a long market presence is not a substitute for a communication strategy. Companies often mistakenly believe that sporadic social media posts or occasional announcements constitute meaningful communication, rather than a consistent effort to answer who they are, what they do well, and why they matter.\n\nThe core issue is not necessarily a need for companies to do more, but to make visible what they already do. Effective business communication should aim to build a consistent perception, answering fundamental questions about identity, core competencies, relevance, and memorability. Without this, the potential value generated internally remains unrecognized externally, hindering market position and growth opportunities.",
      "why": "Businesses in Equatorial Guinea may lose market share and investment opportunities because their internal value and achievements are not effectively communicated to the wider market.",
