      "articleId": "8bbb30d2547b76bcbb0f",
      "headline": "High Court Review Delays Danny Jordaan Fraud Case",
      "dek": "A fraud case against SAFA president Danny Jordaan and co-accused is postponed to November 27, 2026, pending a High Court decision on a review application concerning procedural.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The fraud case involving South African Football Association (SAFA) president Danny Jordaan and four co-accused has been postponed to November 27, 2026. The Specialised Commercial Crimes Court in Palm Ridge delayed the proceedings to await a High Court ruling on a review application. This application challenges the dismissal of an earlier bid to investigate alleged delays under Section 342A of the Criminal Procedure Act. The accused, including former SAFA CFO Grown Hluyo and Grit Communications director Trevor Neethling, face charges of fraud, theft, and conspiracy, with alleged fraud involving over R1 million.",
        "The State, represented by the National Prosecuting Authority (NPA), stated its readiness to proceed with the trial. NPA spokesperson Magaboke Mohlatlole confirmed the State will wait for the High Court's decision before moving forward. Prosecutors allege Jordaan unlawfully signed and backdated a service agreement with Grit Communications in December 2017, bypassing SAFA approval processes to protect his personal image. He is also accused of procuring personal security services disguised as SAFA business.",
        "Co-accused Grown Hluyo allegedly authorised payments to Grit Communications and a security firm without proper SAFA authorisation. Former acting SAFA CEO Russell Paul is accused of colluding in an unlawful 2018 service level agreement with Grit Communications. The NPA reiterated its commitment to concluding the case lawfully and allowing the justice system to function."
      ],
      "body": "The fraud case involving South African Football Association (SAFA) president Danny Jordaan and four co-accused has been postponed to November 27, 2026. The Specialised Commercial Crimes Court in Palm Ridge delayed the proceedings to await a High Court ruling on a review application. This application challenges the dismissal of an earlier bid to investigate alleged delays under Section 342A of the Criminal Procedure Act. The accused, including former SAFA CFO Grown Hluyo and Grit Communications director Trevor Neethling, face charges of fraud, theft, and conspiracy, with alleged fraud involving over R1 million.\n\nThe State, represented by the National Prosecuting Authority (NPA), stated its readiness to proceed with the trial. NPA spokesperson Magaboke Mohlatlole confirmed the State will wait for the High Court's decision before moving forward. Prosecutors allege Jordaan unlawfully signed and backdated a service agreement with Grit Communications in December 2017, bypassing SAFA approval processes to protect his personal image. He is also accused of procuring personal security services disguised as SAFA business.\n\nCo-accused Grown Hluyo allegedly authorised payments to Grit Communications and a security firm without proper SAFA authorisation. Former acting SAFA CEO Russell Paul is accused of colluding in an unlawful 2018 service level agreement with Grit Communications. The NPA reiterated its commitment to concluding the case lawfully and allowing the justice system to function.",
      "why": "The ongoing fraud trial and its delays could impact the reputation and leadership stability of SAFA, potentially affecting business operations and investor confidence in the organization.",
