      "articleId": "7182f36e87b03f50f52e",
      "headline": "Convicted Bosnian Serb war criminal Mladić dies aged 84 in UN prison",
      "dek": "Ratko Mladić, convicted of genocide and war crimes during the 1990s Bosnian War, died in a UN detention facility at The Hague where he was serving a life sentence.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Ratko Mladić, the former Bosnian Serb military commander convicted of genocide and war crimes, has died at the age of 84. He passed away in a UN detention facility at The Hague, where he was serving a life sentence handed down in 2017. Mladić had been receiving palliative care in the prison hospital after his bid for release was rejected in May, with a judge noting his \"dire\" condition but confirming he was receiving \"comprehensive and compassionate treatment.\"",
        "Known as the \"Butcher of Bosnia,\" Mladić commanded Bosnian Serb forces during the 1992-95 wars, leading troops in actions including the siege of Sarajevo and the Srebrenica massacre. He evaded capture for 16 years after the war, until his arrest in rural Serbia in 2011. His conviction for genocide, war crimes, and crimes against humanity was upheld on appeal in 2021.",
        "Reactions to his death varied. Srebrenica survivor Hasan Hasanović stated that Mladić's death offered no closure, as victims could not return and the \"political project\" of genocide denial persisted. Conversely, Bosnian Serb separatist leader Milorad Dodik asserted that Mladić was respected by Serbs and acted to defend them, a sentiment echoed by some Belgrade residents who believed he protected the Serbian people."
      ],
      "body": "Ratko Mladić, the former Bosnian Serb military commander convicted of genocide and war crimes, has died at the age of 84. He passed away in a UN detention facility at The Hague, where he was serving a life sentence handed down in 2017. Mladić had been receiving palliative care in the prison hospital after his bid for release was rejected in May, with a judge noting his \"dire\" condition but confirming he was receiving \"comprehensive and compassionate treatment.\"\n\nKnown as the \"Butcher of Bosnia,\" Mladić commanded Bosnian Serb forces during the 1992-95 wars, leading troops in actions including the siege of Sarajevo and the Srebrenica massacre. He evaded capture for 16 years after the war, until his arrest in rural Serbia in 2011. His conviction for genocide, war crimes, and crimes against humanity was upheld on appeal in 2021.\n\nReactions to his death varied. Srebrenica survivor Hasan Hasanović stated that Mladić's death offered no closure, as victims could not return and the \"political project\" of genocide denial persisted. Conversely, Bosnian Serb separatist leader Milorad Dodik asserted that Mladić was respected by Serbs and acted to defend them, a sentiment echoed by some Belgrade residents who believed he protected the Serbian people.",
      "why": "The death of Ratko Mladić, a convicted war criminal, may impact international relations and historical narratives relevant to diaspora communities with ties to the former Yugoslavia.",
