      "articleId": "c255dfeb5ebfaeda0b59",
      "headline": "Lissu Cross-Examined on Speech Publication in Treason Trial",
      "dek": "The prosecution pressed CHADEMA chairperson Tundu Lissu on remarks made in an April 3, 2025 speech and whether he controlled its online publication, which he denied.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The prosecution began cross-examining CHADEMA chairperson Tundu Lissu on Wednesday in the High Court treason trial, focusing on his April 3, 2025 speech and its online publication. Lissu acknowledged delivering the speech to CHADEMA aspirants and wanting it to reach the public, but he rejected the claim that he controlled the Jambo TV recording's release. He stated a station employee, identified as PW17, made the decision to stream and publish the footage.",
        "The questioning followed a two-day pause in proceedings, including a public holiday for Maulid. Lissu had previously presented his defence, detailing his political activism and arguments for election reform. State Attorney Nassoro Katuga led the prosecution's questioning, establishing Lissu's legal background and CHADEMA chairmanship. The exchange became pointed when Lissu confirmed he had not produced documentary proof for awards he mentioned, stating he had no obligation unless the prosecution challenged his account.",
        "Lissu did not deny making the words attributed to him, including references to \"kukinukisha,\" mobilising rebellion, and stopping the election. His defence maintained that these phrases were taken out of context from a longer speech. The prosecution tested Lissu's stance that citizens can lawfully oppose election laws and government decisions through public mobilisation, a key point in his defence."
      ],
      "body": "The prosecution began cross-examining CHADEMA chairperson Tundu Lissu on Wednesday in the High Court treason trial, focusing on his April 3, 2025 speech and its online publication. Lissu acknowledged delivering the speech to CHADEMA aspirants and wanting it to reach the public, but he rejected the claim that he controlled the Jambo TV recording's release. He stated a station employee, identified as PW17, made the decision to stream and publish the footage.\n\nThe questioning followed a two-day pause in proceedings, including a public holiday for Maulid. Lissu had previously presented his defence, detailing his political activism and arguments for election reform. State Attorney Nassoro Katuga led the prosecution's questioning, establishing Lissu's legal background and CHADEMA chairmanship. The exchange became pointed when Lissu confirmed he had not produced documentary proof for awards he mentioned, stating he had no obligation unless the prosecution challenged his account.\n\nLissu did not deny making the words attributed to him, including references to \"kukinukisha,\" mobilising rebellion, and stopping the election. His defence maintained that these phrases were taken out of context from a longer speech. The prosecution tested Lissu's stance that citizens can lawfully oppose election laws and government decisions through public mobilisation, a key point in his defence.",
