      "articleId": "651bfc33882d8e11808f",
      "headline": "Gabonese Journalist Rudy Hombenet Dies Suddenly After Childbirth",
      "dek": "Rudy Hombenet Anvingui, recognized as the best print journalist in 2026, passed away in Libreville on August 27, just two days after giving birth.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Rudy Hombenet Anvingui, a journalist at the daily L’Union and a recent recipient of the Makongonio 2026 award for best print journalist, died in Libreville on the night of August 26-27. Her death occurred only two months after her professional recognition and shortly after she gave birth to a daughter.",
        "Hombenet, who joined L’Union in 2015, focused her work on social issues, education, and particularly health. Her recent articles explored topics such as tuberculosis, depression, malaria, and hospital operations, bringing daily realities to readers. She had previously expressed a strong aversion to injustice, stating her journalism aimed to give voice to the voiceless.",
        "Her career, spanning over a decade, was marked by a commitment to factual reporting and rigorous investigation. Hombenet held a Master's degree in Communication from Omar-Bongo University. Colleagues remember her for her seriousness, rigor, and dedication to journalistic principles."
      ],
      "body": "Rudy Hombenet Anvingui, a journalist at the daily L’Union and a recent recipient of the Makongonio 2026 award for best print journalist, died in Libreville on the night of August 26-27. Her death occurred only two months after her professional recognition and shortly after she gave birth to a daughter.\n\nHombenet, who joined L’Union in 2015, focused her work on social issues, education, and particularly health. Her recent articles explored topics such as tuberculosis, depression, malaria, and hospital operations, bringing daily realities to readers. She had previously expressed a strong aversion to injustice, stating her journalism aimed to give voice to the voiceless.\n\nHer career, spanning over a decade, was marked by a commitment to factual reporting and rigorous investigation. Hombenet held a Master's degree in Communication from Omar-Bongo University. Colleagues remember her for her seriousness, rigor, and dedication to journalistic principles.",
