      "headline": "Government and NGO Partnership Targets Domestic Abuse Fatalities",
      "dek": "A civil society proposal advocates for a formal collaboration between government bodies and non-governmental organizations to enhance early risk identification and intervention.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A new policy proposal from FAM [Fam an Mars] suggests a strengthened, formal partnership between the Mauritian Government and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) to prevent domestic abuse fatalities. The proposal, prepared for government consideration, aims to shift focus from responding to abuse after it occurs to proactively identifying risks, coordinating protection, and ultimately preventing deaths.",
        "Mauritius has updated its legal framework with the Domestic Abuse Act 2026, which broadens the definition of abuse to include psychological, emotional, economic, digital, and coercive forms. The government has also established frontline Family Support Services and continues to train first responders. However, the proposal highlights that legislation alone is insufficient to avert all tragedies.",
        "The core challenge addressed is the ability of government and civil society to collectively identify high-risk families early, share information effectively, and intervene before domestic violence escalates to severe injury or death. The proposed partnership seeks to bridge this gap by formalizing collaboration between the Ministry of Gender Equality and Family Welfare and competent NGOs."
      ],
      "body": "A new policy proposal from FAM [Fam an Mars] suggests a strengthened, formal partnership between the Mauritian Government and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) to prevent domestic abuse fatalities. The proposal, prepared for government consideration, aims to shift focus from responding to abuse after it occurs to proactively identifying risks, coordinating protection, and ultimately preventing deaths.\n\nMauritius has updated its legal framework with the Domestic Abuse Act 2026, which broadens the definition of abuse to include psychological, emotional, economic, digital, and coercive forms. The government has also established frontline Family Support Services and continues to train first responders. However, the proposal highlights that legislation alone is insufficient to avert all tragedies.\n\nThe core challenge addressed is the ability of government and civil society to collectively identify high-risk families early, share information effectively, and intervene before domestic violence escalates to severe injury or death. The proposed partnership seeks to bridge this gap by formalizing collaboration between the Ministry of Gender Equality and Family Welfare and competent NGOs.",
      "why": "This initiative aims to protect individuals experiencing domestic abuse by improving early detection and intervention, potentially saving lives.",
