      "headline": "Government Buys N$40m in Local Grain to Support Farmers",
      "dek": "Prime Minister Elijah Ngurare announced a N$40 million government purchase of grain from local farmers to address market access issues and mitigate post-harvest losses.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The Namibian government has allocated N$40 million to purchase grain directly from local farmers through the Agricultural Marketing and Trading Agency (AMTA). Prime Minister Elijah Ngurare stated the funds will be channelled via the finance and agriculture ministries to support crop producers facing challenges accessing reliable markets. This intervention aims to mitigate post-harvest losses and ensure farmers benefit from their yields, addressing issues like weak domestic processing and insufficient storage.",
        "Farmers have recently struggled to find buyers for their substantial harvests, with regions like Zambezi reporting large yields but only one commercial miller. This disconnect between production and market access, exacerbated by a lack of coordinated off-take, has led to fears of waste. The government's move is also part of a broader drought relief program in anticipation of the El Niño weather pattern, which threatens extreme agricultural drought and water scarcity.",
        "The government assured farmers that systems are in place to procure all available grains. Prime Minister Ngurare confirmed that this support is designed to assist those with bumper harvests who are struggling to sell their produce. Additionally, the Ministry of Education, Innovation, Youth, Sport, Arts and Culture has been instructed to incorporate locally produced food into the national school feeding programme."
      ],
      "body": "The Namibian government has allocated N$40 million to purchase grain directly from local farmers through the Agricultural Marketing and Trading Agency (AMTA). Prime Minister Elijah Ngurare stated the funds will be channelled via the finance and agriculture ministries to support crop producers facing challenges accessing reliable markets. This intervention aims to mitigate post-harvest losses and ensure farmers benefit from their yields, addressing issues like weak domestic processing and insufficient storage.\n\nFarmers have recently struggled to find buyers for their substantial harvests, with regions like Zambezi reporting large yields but only one commercial miller. This disconnect between production and market access, exacerbated by a lack of coordinated off-take, has led to fears of waste. The government's move is also part of a broader drought relief program in anticipation of the El Niño weather pattern, which threatens extreme agricultural drought and water scarcity.\n\nThe government assured farmers that systems are in place to procure all available grains. Prime Minister Ngurare confirmed that this support is designed to assist those with bumper harvests who are struggling to sell their produce. Additionally, the Ministry of Education, Innovation, Youth, Sport, Arts and Culture has been instructed to incorporate locally produced food into the national school feeding programme.",
