      "articleId": "5d1c4d6b6af36b022759",
      "headline": "SA men seek M202,000 return in drug case bail bid",
      "dek": "Two South African men charged with cannabis possession and money laundering have applied for bail, demanding the return of M202,000 cash and a seized vehicle.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Nash Pillay and Thabiso Ngidi, facing cannabis possession and money laundering charges, have filed a fresh bail application in the Maseru Magistrate’s Court. Their initial bid was rejected on July 9, 2026, leading to their remand. Pillay, who identifies as a herbal doctor and entrepreneur with a South African company, claims cannabis is a vital medicinal herb in his Muslim tradition. The pair are jointly seeking M1,000 bail each.",
        "The proposed bail conditions include Ngidi reporting to Maseru Central Police Station weekly and surrendering his passport. They also demand the return of M202,000 confiscated from Pillay on July 7, 2026, or that it be held in trust by their lawyers. They also seek the release of a seized motor vehicle. The accused state a seized pill is a dental painkiller, not an illicit drug, and request its return for Ngidi’s urgent medical treatment.",
        "The defense argues the accused are presumed innocent and not flight risks due to their South African ties and lack of prior convictions. They challenge the seizure of cash and vehicle, claiming police lacked reasonable suspicion and that the funds were for legitimate business. The State opposes the application, citing a lack of valid licenses and flight risk concerns, as detailed by investigating officer Detective Police Constable No. 10709 Paki."
      ],
      "body": "Nash Pillay and Thabiso Ngidi, facing cannabis possession and money laundering charges, have filed a fresh bail application in the Maseru Magistrate’s Court. Their initial bid was rejected on July 9, 2026, leading to their remand. Pillay, who identifies as a herbal doctor and entrepreneur with a South African company, claims cannabis is a vital medicinal herb in his Muslim tradition. The pair are jointly seeking M1,000 bail each.\n\nThe proposed bail conditions include Ngidi reporting to Maseru Central Police Station weekly and surrendering his passport. They also demand the return of M202,000 confiscated from Pillay on July 7, 2026, or that it be held in trust by their lawyers. They also seek the release of a seized motor vehicle. The accused state a seized pill is a dental painkiller, not an illicit drug, and request its return for Ngidi’s urgent medical treatment.\n\nThe defense argues the accused are presumed innocent and not flight risks due to their South African ties and lack of prior convictions. They challenge the seizure of cash and vehicle, claiming police lacked reasonable suspicion and that the funds were for legitimate business. The State opposes the application, citing a lack of valid licenses and flight risk concerns, as detailed by investigating officer Detective Police Constable No. 10709 Paki.",
