      "articleId": "8b5421c8a2096f93a9b5",
      "headline": "Refugee Documents Freeze Strands Hundreds in Lesotho",
      "dek": "A year-long government freeze on identity and travel documents leaves refugees unable to access essential services, including healthcare and banking.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Hundreds of refugees in Lesotho are effectively trapped, unable to travel or access basic services due to a government-imposed freeze on identity and travel documents. This administrative crisis, now entering its second year, stems from an investigation into alleged corruption in the issuance of refugee certificates. Refugees report being unable to access healthcare, banking services, and professional opportunities, while critically ill patients struggle to get referrals to South Africa.",
        "The suspension of document issuance began in July last year, following allegations of corruption against the former Acting Commissioner for Refugees and the Director of Passports. Both officials were suspended and later charged with corruption and abuse of power. Although investigations by the Directorate on Corruption and Economic Offences have concluded, the case awaits reallocation in the Magistrates Court after a magistrate's retirement.",
        "Despite the completion of investigations, refugees remain without the fundamental documents required for daily life, work, and study. The continued freeze raises questions about compliance with Lesotho's refugee laws and international obligations, which mandate the issuance of identity cards and resident permits to recognised refugees. The situation leaves families stranded and bank accounts frozen, impacting the most vulnerable."
      ],
      "body": "Hundreds of refugees in Lesotho are effectively trapped, unable to travel or access basic services due to a government-imposed freeze on identity and travel documents. This administrative crisis, now entering its second year, stems from an investigation into alleged corruption in the issuance of refugee certificates. Refugees report being unable to access healthcare, banking services, and professional opportunities, while critically ill patients struggle to get referrals to South Africa.\n\nThe suspension of document issuance began in July last year, following allegations of corruption against the former Acting Commissioner for Refugees and the Director of Passports. Both officials were suspended and later charged with corruption and abuse of power. Although investigations by the Directorate on Corruption and Economic Offences have concluded, the case awaits reallocation in the Magistrates Court after a magistrate's retirement.\n\nDespite the completion of investigations, refugees remain without the fundamental documents required for daily life, work, and study. The continued freeze raises questions about compliance with Lesotho's refugee laws and international obligations, which mandate the issuance of identity cards and resident permits to recognised refugees. The situation leaves families stranded and bank accounts frozen, impacting the most vulnerable.",
      "why": "Refugees are unable to access essential services like healthcare and banking, and are unable to travel for treatment or opportunities.",
